      "writtenOn": "2025-05-14T16:51:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This tests that the EC conveys the discovery information faithfully from the partner/cable to the AP, but that should already be covered by existing tests. The more important thing to test here is that we enter DP mode properly in the presence of both SVDM 2.0/1.0 and SVDM 2.1. You can see tests of mode entry for various cable types in `usbc_dp_mode.c`, where we check the mux state at the end of the mode entry sequence as as proxy for mode entry having worked.\n\nLooking through those mode entry tests reveals some gaps in the existing tests. We don\u0027t necessarily have to cover them right now, but we might end up covering some of them incidentally for the purpose of this CL. I\u0027ll list them here for discussion:\n1. The existing tests check DP mode entry by checking the mux state. However, the process is different at the protocol level between DPAM 2.1 and 2.0. Specifically, the TCPM should attempt to Enter Mode on SOP\u0027 and maybe SOP\u0027\u0027 for cables supporting DPAM 2.1. In DPAM \u003c2.1, cable mode entry is implicit. Therefore, we should expect to see different cable traffic in the message log between DPAM 2.1 and older.\n   Right now, we\u0027re not checking that traffic at all.\n2. DPAM 2.1 cables are defined to fall back gracefully to implicit cable mode entry when the partner does not explicitly Enter Mode on SOP\u0027/SOP\u0027\u0027. In the existing tests, I think this is the code path that we\u0027re exercising, because the existing tests don\u0027t define cable Enter Mode/Status/Config ACKs. I expect that if we look at the message logs for the DPAM 2.1 tests, we\u0027ll see Enter Mode NAKs from the cable and the TCPM moving on.\n   We should also be exercising the path where the cable ACKs Enter Mode and checking that the traffic makes sense.",
      "fixSuggestions": [
